---
description: Enforces rigorous test-driven development with AAA pattern, edge case coverage, and proper mocking boundaries. Activate when writing, reviewing, or generating tests.
globs: ["**/*Test.java", "**/*test*.py", "**/*.test.ts", "**/*.spec.ts", "**/test_*.py", "tests/**"]
alwaysApply: false
---
<🎯 Title>
TDD & Unit Testing Instructions

## Rules
- ALWAYS use strict Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) layout — visually separated with blank lines.
- ALWAYS follow test naming conventions: `should_ExpectedBehavior_When_Condition` (Java), `test_expected_when_condition` (Python), or `it('should ... when ...')` (JS/TS).
- MANDATORY negative tests for every public method: null inputs, empty collections, boundary values, unauthorized access, timeouts.
- NEVER mock the class/unit under test — only mock external I/O boundaries (DB, HTTP, filesystem).
- NEVER use `@SuppressWarnings` or `@Ignore`/`@Disabled` without an expiration date and ticket.
- ALWAYS have one assertion concept per test (multiple assertions OK if testing the same logical concept).
- ALWAYS use test data builders or factory methods — NEVER hardcode magic strings/numbers without constants.
- ALWAYS include Given-When-Then comments for BDD-style tests.
- ALWAYS test error messages, not just exception types.
- PREFER `assertThat()` (AssertJ/Hamcrest) over `assertEquals()` for readability.
- ALWAYS aim for test coverage targets: > 80% line coverage AND > 60% mutation score.
- NEVER write tests that pass without the implementation (avoid tautological tests).

## Verification
- Run `mvn test`, `pytest --tb=short`, `npm test -- --coverage` to verify your code.
